{"title":"Three Greek problems of antiquity","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The <b>three Greek problems of antiquity</b> were some of the most famous unsolved problems in history. They were first posed by the Greeks but were not settled till the advent of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Abstract_algebra\" title=\"Abstract algebra\">Abstract algebra</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Analysis\" title=\"Analysis\">Analysis</a> in modern times. \n</p><p>All three constructions have been shown to be impossible. \n</p><p>The impossibility of these constructions relies on the characterization of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Constructible_number\" title=\"Constructible number\">constructible numbers</a>. It can be shown that, starting with a segment of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, one can construct a segment of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> can be formed from the rational numbers in a finite number of steps using only the operations addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, and taking square roots. Using <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Field_theory\" title=\"Field theory\">field theory</a>, one can then show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> must be an <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Algebraic_number\" title=\"Algebraic number\">algebraic number</a> and that the degree of it's <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Minimal_polynomial\" title=\"Minimal polynomial\">minimal polynomial</a> must be a power of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Trisection_of_the_General_Angle\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Trisection of the General Angle</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Doubling_of_a_Cube\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Doubling of a Cube</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Squaring_the_Circle\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Squaring the Circle</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Trisection_of_the_General_Angle\">Trisection of the General Angle</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Three_Greek_problems_of_antiquity&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Trisection of the General Angle\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Statement: Given an angle, construct by means of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Straight_edge\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Straight edge\">straight edge</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Compass\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Compass\">compass</a> only, an angle one-third its measure. \n</p><p>This is impossible because the number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/b/57b98d5206ee9bb579b467e6dff0ca3cbd8a87b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos 20^{\\circ}$\" width=\"51\" height=\"13\" /> would have to be constructible in this case, and the minimal polynomial of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/b/57b98d5206ee9bb579b467e6dff0ca3cbd8a87b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\cos 20^{\\circ}$\" width=\"51\" height=\"13\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/0/da0725b31c7782c2aee476b925cff829a0888734.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8x^3-6x-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"99\" height=\"15\" />, which has degree <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>You can also go to this website: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"http://www.murderousmaths.co.uk/books/MMoE/trisect.htm\">http://www.murderousmaths.co.uk/books/MMoE/trisect.htm</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Doubling_of_a_Cube\">Doubling of a Cube</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Three_Greek_problems_of_antiquity&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Doubling of a Cube\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Statement: Given a cube, construct by means of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Straight_edge\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Straight edge\">straight edge</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Compass\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Compass\">compass</a> only, a cube with double the volume. \n</p><p>This is impossible because the number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/d/9fdfe572d1673f00f286d5ed753942d18f401cbb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt[3]{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"25\" height=\"18\" /> would have to be constructed, and the minimal polynomial of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/d/9fdfe572d1673f00f286d5ed753942d18f401cbb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt[3]{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"25\" height=\"18\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/f/42fa82d48c79d592f351f69284404b208cb54702.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^3-2$\" width=\"48\" height=\"15\" />, which has degree <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Just buy the book: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"http://www.murderousmaths.co.uk/books/MMoE.htm\">http://www.murderousmaths.co.uk/books/MMoE.htm</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Squaring_the_Circle\">Squaring the Circle</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Three_Greek_problems_of_antiquity&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Squaring the Circle\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Statement: Given a circle, construct by means of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Straight_edge\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Straight edge\">straight edge</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Compass\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Compass\">compass</a> only, a square with area same as that of the circle.\n</p><p>This is impossible because you will have to construct <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/0/ee0529202bae221e2a886dbd92ecd50e87f90452.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{\\pi}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"26\" height=\"18\" />, which is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Transcendental_number\" title=\"Transcendental number\">transcendental number</a>.\n</p><p>You can go to this website: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"http://www.murderousmaths.co.uk/books/MMoE/sqcirc.htm\">http://www.murderousmaths.co.uk/books/MMoE/sqcirc.htm</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Three_Greek_problems_of_antiquity&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Construction\" title=\"Construction\">Construction</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pi\" title=\"Pi\">Pi</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Transcendental_number\" title=\"Transcendental number\">Transcendental number</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Irrational_number\" title=\"Irrational number\">Irrational number</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Field_theory\" title=\"Field theory\">Field theory</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018112145\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.018 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.029 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 95/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 2378/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:6324:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018112145 and revision id 174681. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}